 "kind": "statement",
 "title": "P8: the Fourier trace formula",
 "statement": "For $y\\in\\mathbf F_{q^n}$ and a Weil complex $K$ on $\\Aone$,\n\n$$\nt_{\\FT_\\psi(K)}(y)\n=-\\sum_{x\\in\\mathbf F_{q^n}}t_K(x)\\,\\psi\\bigl(\\Tr_{\\mathbf F_{q^n}/k_0}(xy)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nThus, up to the sign coming from the shift $[1]$, the trace function of the\ntransform is the classical Fourier transform of the trace function of the\ninput.",
 "source": {
  "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-fourier-transform/sk-thm-fourier-trace-formula/_section.md",
  "place": "node sk-thm-fourier-trace-formula, order 3050"
 },
 "stmt_deps": [
  "sk-def-trace"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "By P3 the stalk of $\\FT_\\psi(K)$ at the geometric point over $y$ is\n$H^*_c(\\Aone,K\\otimes^L\\mathcal L_{\\psi(xy)})[1]$. The\nGrothendieck–Lefschetz trace formula over $\\mathbf F_{q^n}$\n(thm:trf-grothendieck-lefschetz-qbar in the point-counting form\ncor:trf-point-counting) computes the trace of the Frobenius on that\ncomplex as the sum of the traces at the geometric points of the fibre, i.e. as $-\\sum_xt_{K\\otimes^L\\mathcal L_{\\psi(xy)}}(x)$, the sign being the shift\n$[1]$ of [sk-def-ft]. The trace of a tensor product is the product of the\ntraces, and the trace of the Artin–Schreier factor at $x$ is\n$\\psi(\\Tr_{\\mathbf F_{q^n}/k_0}(xy))$ by [sk-lem-as-properties].",
   "uses": [
    "sk-def-ft",
    "sk-lem-as-properties"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  }
 ],
 "proof_deps": [
